WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.790
- Gary Vee, welcome to The Mentor.

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:03.880
- Thank you for having me.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:04.880
- Gary's in the house!

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:05.713
That's pretty cool.

00:00:05.713 --> 00:00:06.770
- Yes.
- That's very cool for me,

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:07.800
let me tell you.
- Thank you.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.827
- I'm a big fan of yours.
- Thank you so much.

00:00:09.827 --> 00:00:12.240
- So, what we like to do
for our audience at least,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.210
is that our audience
loves to know a little bit

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.230
about the person.

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.330
I mean, you know, you're a public figure.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:18.990
Everybody probably
follows you on Instagram

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.240
and all the various other places

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.650
that you put out your material.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.390
They probably don't get that
much time to hear really

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:25.640
about the Gary Vee story.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.300
- Sure.
- Who is Gary Vaynerchuk?

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.190
I'd like to know.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:29.540
- (sighing) You know,
- Tell me.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:33.580
- It's a very classic
immigrant story, you know,

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.810
of a boy that's born in a
deeply communist country

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:40.740
like the Soviet Union, coming to America,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.760
which is a very capitalist
country, in the late 70's.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.140
Unfortunately there was
not a very good economy

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.340
at the time when we came,
so these golden streets

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:53.920
that my parents were promised

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.520
were even worse than they were in Russia.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.360
So much so that some fun
stories have popped up recently

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.560
in my family of like,
even debating to go back.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.393
- Seriously?
- Yeah.

00:01:03.393 --> 00:01:05.140
Which was kinda interesting even for me.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.830
So, it was a lot of struggle.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:11.580
You know, I always tell a
lot of my friends that I'm

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.420
far more similar to their grandfather

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:17.210
than I am to them, 'cause I
was born in the old country.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:18.600
Because I came over.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.310
Because I worked all the time.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.430
Because there was no entitlement.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:27.095
School was kind of
almost weirdly secondary,

00:01:27.095 --> 00:01:28.758
in a lot of ways.

00:01:28.758 --> 00:01:29.591
- School in America, or school in...

00:01:29.591 --> 00:01:31.100
- Yeah, school in America.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:33.230
You know, I wasn't a good student.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:37.790
And you know, for a lot of
second and third generations

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.760
school is the big play, but
for a lot of first generations

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.520
you're just trying to
put food on the table

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:44.610
and that's what my parents did,

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:48.820
but I was so intertwined
with them because I was born

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.070
in the old world.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.790
And it was a very entrepreneurial story.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.220
By the time I was six or seven years old

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.440
I was selling lemonade,
I was shoveling snow,

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.270
I was washing cars, I was selling flowers.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.366
It was...
- What drove that?

00:02:01.366 --> 00:02:03.020
Why, why?
- DNA.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:03.853
There's nothing else.

00:02:03.853 --> 00:02:05.460
- You didn't have to put
food on your own table?

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.430
- I did not, it was just
more fun for me than playing.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:13.320
It was more fun for me when
snow would hit the ground

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.500
to go in the garage, grab a shovel

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.100
and ring people's door bells and say

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.308
I will shovel your driveway
for $5, than it was

00:02:20.308 --> 00:02:24.250
to build a snowman or
sledding or throw snowballs

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:25.083
at each other.

00:02:25.083 --> 00:02:26.190
- Was it a game?

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.720
- It continues to be a game.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.680
- Yeah, so even then it was a game then?

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:30.930
- Hundred percent.
- Work was a game?

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:31.800
- Yes.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.040
And an enjoyable game.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.040
I think one of the reasons I'm successful

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.610
and one of the things I
try to push a lot of people

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.610
to think about is, can
you love your process?

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.290
You know, if you're in this,

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:44.217
and entrepreneurship is cool
now, if you're in this...

00:02:44.217 --> 00:02:45.910
- Too cool.
- Too cool.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.160
That's exactly right.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.943
If you're in this to flex on
Instagram you're gonna lose.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:55.170
And I was doing it when it
was in the 80's and 90's

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:59.110
in America, entrepreneurship
wasn't a thing.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:00.890
- In this country people
thought it was somethin' bad.

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:02.680
- And America too.
- You were a bad dude.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:03.870
- If you're an entrepreneur
- Risk.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:07.053
- you're actually a loser
living off of Mommy and Daddy.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.270
You're exactly right.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.100
You and I know this, a lot
of the kids don't know.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.260
Now it's like being a
rapper or an athlete.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.790
It's incredible for me to
watch what's transformed

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:18.560
over 30 years.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.200
But I was willing to do it
because it's all I could do.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:27.200
I listened to athletes,
actors and actresses, singers,

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:30.560
artists, and in their
documentaries inevitably

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.860
they're like, "Well I just
couldn't do anything else".

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:37.123
And as an entrepreneur that's how I think.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.620
- You know, there's a lot of

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.003
hard luck stories that
I hear around the world.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.090
Mike Tyson's a good example.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:48.630
He had nothin' and he
used sport as his way out.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.260
Do you think entrepreneurship
is a way out?

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:52.860
- You know, I think--
- Then, back then.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.323
- I think it is for some, I do.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.340
I don't think it was for me.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.390
I didn't feel desperate, I didn't grow up

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:02.850
in an environment that scared me.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:03.980
- [Host] Didn't you have choices?

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:06.050
- I did have choices, to be frank, I did.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.760
I could've decided to be a good student.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.300
I just loved it.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:13.590
I love it now.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.120
I love it.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.860
I mean, on the flight here from the US,

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:22.660
I woke up 1 a.m. Sydney time,
knowing that it was probably

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.320
in my best interests to
sleep a little bit more

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.940
to get on time here, but I
was so happy that I woke up

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.470
because I couldn't wait
to go on eBay and look up

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:35.570
the value of sports cards,
because in the last month,

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.450
and really over the last
year but in the last month,

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:38.990
I've gotten white hot in the theory that

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.240
sports cards are about to make a comeback,

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:44.510
and I wanna buy up as many
soccer and basketball cards

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:47.020
as I can, 'cause I think
they're gonna go up a bunch.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:52.020
That's an energy that, and
I'm gonna make no money,

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:55.520
I'll make more money speaking
in this four city tour

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.460
during this week than I
will in the countless hours

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:01.230
I put in over the next
year, trying to buy up

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:04.360
Messi rookie cards or LeBron rookie cards

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:07.243
but the action of the game, the,

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.260
something that nobody else saw.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.980
I'm far more proud that I
saw social media, Facebook,

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:18.450
Twitter, those things,
than the money I made

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.090
by making those bets.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:21.791
- Can I ask you something?

00:05:21.791 --> 00:05:23.600
You're an intense dude.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:24.920
- Yes. (chuckles)
- You are.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:29.070
That's cool, I'm similar,
but in a different way.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.970
And a lot of people don't like it, okay?

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:32.803
But I can just see you talking about

00:05:32.803 --> 00:05:35.110
just like the cards now.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:38.763
What is it in the fabric
of Gary Vaynerchuk's life,

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:40.930
you're going to say it's in your DNA,

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:43.440
but what is it that in
your social development

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.990
that actually was formed all the layers

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:47.220
to get you to be so intense?

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.830
Because you can't be good at what you do

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:49.663
unless you're intense.

00:05:49.663 --> 00:05:51.720
You gotta love it, get it,
but you gotta be intense.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.348
- I think--
- Think intensely.

00:05:53.348 --> 00:05:54.348
- I think...

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:59.263
Being on your own, you know.

00:06:01.792 --> 00:06:05.260
Not having the luxury,

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.130
which I think is a disadvantage,

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:09.280
of an over-coddling nature.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.610
- [Host] What does that
mean, doing it on your own?

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.430
- You know, as soon as I was
four, five, six years old

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.130
I'm outside playing by myself.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:17.037
- No brothers and sisters?

00:06:17.037 --> 00:06:19.290
- I did, but you know, they
were three years younger

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.610
and eleven years younger,
so at first it's me.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:23.480
So, no brothers and sisters.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.200
A mother that grew up in the Soviet Union

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.870
where the framework was
go outside and play.

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:31.560
The 80's when you could
still do that in America.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.990
So, crossing the street,
playing in the woods,

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:37.190
I mean, there's 15 year
olds that are being watched

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:39.524
by their parents more than I
was watched when I was five.

00:06:39.524 --> 00:06:40.357
- Fucking kills me.

00:06:40.357 --> 00:06:42.030
- Of course it kills you,
'cause you know exactly

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:42.963
the vulnerability.

00:06:44.105 --> 00:06:46.853
We're living in a time where
everyone's a zoo animal.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:49.770
They don't know how to live
in society 'cause parents

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:53.050
are such helicopters. and
eighth place trophies,

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:54.900
and nobody's allowed to lose,

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.491
nobody's allowed to cry.
- I saw that on Instagram,

00:06:56.491 --> 00:06:57.944
10th place
- Saw that?

00:06:57.944 --> 00:06:59.430
(host mumbles)

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:04.033
So, for me, I think what's in
my fabric is, having to scrap.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:06.773
I'm from the dirt.

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:09.700
I had no choice.

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:10.533
I fight.

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:12.670
- And does that build
your intensity though?

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:13.503
- Of course.

00:07:13.503 --> 00:07:14.919
- And does it ever wear you out?

00:07:14.919 --> 00:07:16.470
- Not for me.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:17.930
You know, I'm empathetic

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:19.220
that it might be too much for others.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:20.070
I respect that.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:23.010
I understand that, I just
also can't live my life

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.390
based on other people's judgment.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:27.350
- Is Mum or Dad energetic like that?

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:29.210
- You know, in different ways yes.

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:32.940
But, neither like come
across in the way that I do.

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:36.110
I think I'm a pretty
interesting blend of the two.

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:40.380
I'm much more of a showman and
high-energy than my father,

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:44.560
but I'm deeply competitive
and in the work ethic.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.730
My mom is not a showman either

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:49.670
but her side of the family
is, especially the men.

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:54.470
So, no, you know, I'm
different than both my parents.

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:57.040
But obviously 'cause it's
so easy for me to read,

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.390
I understand the pieces
of the puzzle that I got

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:01.867
from both of them to how this happened.

00:08:01.867 --> 00:08:03.200
- Are they proud of you?

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:04.123
- Oh, immensely.

00:08:05.597 --> 00:08:06.450
- How important is that to you?

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:09.250
- Oh, unbelievably.

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:12.530
I'm a uncomfortable
advocate of my parents.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:16.113
I'm a deeply grateful child.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:20.160
I mean I'm writing a book in my own head

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:23.567
that I eventually will put out
called "Perfectly Parented".

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:26.840
You know, most people hate their parents

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:31.450
or dwell on their parents,
which is probably not true

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:34.660
but spend a lot of time judging

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:37.130
what their parents did wrong,
instead of thinking about

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:38.160
what their parents did right.

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.800
I'm sure my parents did
tons of things wrong,

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.580
I'm just incapable of focusing on that,

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:45.130
I'm just so grateful for
the things they did right.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:49.180
- There's a lot of people out
there, I mean, you're a doer.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:51.760
The intensity brings
effort and with intensity

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.430
you get a lotta execution.

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:56.187
But, were you a dreamer as a kid?

00:08:56.187 --> 00:08:57.930
Did you sit around dreaming?

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.330
'Cause I mean, you're gonna sell lemonade

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:00.163
or whatever you're gonna do.

00:09:00.163 --> 00:09:00.996
Did you sit down and plan it?

00:09:00.996 --> 00:09:01.917
Like you woke up one--
- Yes.

00:09:01.917 --> 00:09:02.750
- thinking about your cards.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:04.034
Did you do that as a kid?

00:09:04.034 --> 00:09:06.610
- (sighing) Only in the most macro level.

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:09.670
I very early on decided
that I was gonna buy

00:09:09.670 --> 00:09:12.300
my favorite football
team, the New York Jets.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:16.470
And I've stuck to it for
35 years at this point.

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:18.590
So, yeah, I would say I dreamt about that.

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:22.040
I dreamt about making a
mark in the wine business

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:24.830
and building one of the large
retail companies in America.

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:25.800
I did that.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:29.900
I dreamt, I thought, about
disrupting the ad world

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:31.660
in a way that they couldn't see coming.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.790
I'm putting the final
pieces on that right now.

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:39.760
So, I dream currently of
leaving an incredible legacy

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:44.110
and having thousands of
people show up to my funeral

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:47.100
because of the value I
provided with no ask in return.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:49.690
So, yeah, I dream, I dream.

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:54.690
But, I'm too grounded in
practicality to be a talker.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:59.940
You know, I would be embarrassed
to talk and not execute.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:03.000
I almost put things
out to put the pressure

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.980
on making sure I execute,

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.280
so that nobody can ever play a clip,

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.857
'cause I record everything,
of, "You talked a big game

00:10:09.857 --> 00:10:10.690
"but you couldn't do it".

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:13.080
Now, I'm fine, unbelievably fine

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:15.210
with not achieving my goals.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:17.430
I'm not fine with not trying to.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:18.616
- You gotta have a crack.

00:10:18.616 --> 00:10:19.600
A good hard crack.
- Gotta have,

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.576
like, a real fucking crack.
- Yeah, totally.

00:10:21.576 --> 00:10:22.409
- You know what I mean?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:10:22.409 --> 00:10:24.500
- Like, one of the best cracks.

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:26.500
Like that I can feel.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:28.750
I love losing, believe it
or not, because that means

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:29.930
I deserved it.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:33.140
But, I love the way you
delivered that sentence.

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:34.460
It excited me.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:38.340
A real crack, an all time
crack, a last breath fuck it

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:41.410
I did everything, I
went as hard as I could.

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:42.360
- There's nothing left.

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:43.370
- There's nothing left.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:45.620
I wish there was a couple
more years 'cause I'd still be

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:48.780
cracking it a hundred, but if this is it,

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:49.900
I don't want to regret.

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:50.830
I don't want to regret.

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:53.100
And you have to be careful
because a lot of times

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:55.850
one part of your life you
may leave no regret you know,

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:57.590
I spend a lot of time
tryin' to be thoughtful

00:10:57.590 --> 00:11:00.110
of not being a man of the
world, at the expense of

